# Line endings are load-bearing here.
#
# run.sh is the documented entry point and claims Git-Bash support. Without an
# explicit rule, a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true rewrites it to CRLF
# and bash rejects it before the first command:
#
#     ./run.sh: line 8: set: pipefail\r: invalid option name
#
# The index was already LF; nothing enforced it on checkout. Every commit in
# this repo printed "LF will be replaced by CRLF" and it was treated as noise.

# Default: LF in the index AND in the working tree, for every text file.
#
# This was `* text=auto`, which normalizes the index but leaves checkout to
# core.autocrlf -- so each rule below only protected the extensions someone had
# thought of. Adding evidence/*.txt immediately produced the same
# "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warning that caused F15 in the first place.
# Enumerating extensions is whack-a-mole; the default closes the class.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Anything executed by a shell MUST be LF on every platform.
*.sh            text eol=lf
Makefile        text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf
.env.example    text eol=lf

# Go tooling and the container runner both expect LF.
*.go     text eol=lf
go.mod   text eol=lf
go.sum   text eol=lf
*.yml    text eol=lf
*.yaml   text eol=lf

# Fixtures are compared byte-for-byte and hashed in corpus/manifest.json.
# A CRLF rewrite would silently invalidate every recorded SHA-256.
*.jsonl  text eol=lf
*.json   text eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf
*.py     text eol=lf
*.txt    text eol=lf

# Binaries must be exempt from the eol=lf default above.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.pdf binary
*.ico binary
*.db  binary
